                        "response": {
                          "result": "--- GENERATIVE SUMMARY ---\nEligible employees accrue vacation time based on their years of continuous service [1]. For the first five years of employment, employees accrue 3.077 hours biweekly, which amounts to a maximum of 10 days per year [1]. From year six through year ten, the accrual rate increases to 4.62 hours biweekly, allowing for a maximum of 15 days per year [1].\n\nThe total amount of accrued vacation time is capped at twice the annual vacation allotment [1]. Once an employee reaches this cap, further accruals will cease [1]. Accruals will resume once the employee takes vacation time and their accrued balance falls below the cap [1].\n\nIn general, California employers are not legally required to provide paid vacation to their employees [1]. If employers grant Paid Time Off (PTO), they should not have a separate vacation policy [1].\n\n--- EXACT VERBATIM CITATIONS ---\nVerbatim quote: \"Employers who grant PTO should not have a separate vacation policy. In either event, in general, California employers are not required by law to provide paid vacation to their employees.\"\n(Source: DRAFT, Page 37)\nVerbatim quote: \"Accrual Eligible employees accrue vacation in accordance with the following scheduleix: Years of Continuous Service Rate of Accrual Date of hire through end of year 5 3.077 hours biweekly, for a maximum of 10 days per year Years 6 through 10 4.62 hours biweekly, for a maximum of 15 days per year.\"\n(Source: DRAFT, Page 13)\nVerbatim quote: \"The total amount of accrued vacation time may not exceed twice the annual vacation allotment. Once an employee has reached this cap, all further accruals will cease. Vacation accruals will recommence after the employee has taken vacation and the accrued vacation has dropped below the cap.\"\n(Source: DRAFT, Page 14)"
